Hello! Yes, each element of the power set of primes does indeed contain a distinct set of primes. The power set of a set is the set of all possible subsets, including the empty set and the set itself. So, for any set of prime numbers, the power set will include every possible combination of those primes, from the empty set (which contains no primes) to the set containing all the primes.

For example, if we consider the set of the first three prime numbers, {2, 3, 5}, the power set would be:

- {}
- {2}
- {3}
- {5}
- {2, 3}
- {2, 5}
- {3, 5}
- {2, 3, 5}

Each of these subsets is distinct, and each subset is a set of primes (or no primes at all, in the case of the empty set).
